mmunity-associated methicillin-resistant <italic>Staphylococcus aureus</italic> clones from rural Wisconsin.</article-title><source>J Clin Microbiol</source> <year>2004</year>;<volume>42</volume>:<fpage>3752</fpage>&#x02013;<lpage>7</lpage> <pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1128/JCM.42.8.3752-3757.2004</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15297526</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="R15"><label>15. </label><citation citation-type="journal"><string-name><surname>Berglund</surname> <given-names>C</given-names></string-name>, <string-name><surname>Molling</surname> <given-names>P</given-names></string-name>, <string-name><surname>Sjoberg</surname> <given-names>L</given-names></string-name>, <string-name><surname>Soderquist</surname> <given-names>B</given-names></string-name> <article-title>Predominance of staphylococcal cassette chromosome <italic>mec</italic> (SCC<italic>mec</italic>) type IV among methicillin-resistant <italic>Staphylococcus aureus</italic> (MRSA) in a Swedish county and presence of unknown SCC<italic>mec</italic> types with Panton-Valentine leukocidin genes.</article-title><source>Clin Microbiol Infect</source> <year>2005</year>;<volume>11</volume>:<fpage>447</fpage>&#x02013;<lpage>56</lpage> <pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1111/j.1469-0691.2005.01150.x</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15882194</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>